  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Minimal pre-Thanksgiving travel weather impacts \n\nLowering inversion heights in response to eastward migrating \n1030+mb high pressure will lead to a 24-36 hour period of \nquiet/dry weather and good holiday travel conditions. Tuesday \nnight may be the coldest night of the season so far as mainly \nclear, Beaver Supermoon-lit skies and light winds will allow min\ntemps to fall into the 15 to 25F range. Wednesday will be\npleasant with increasing clouds as the outer edges of the \ncoastal storm begin their approach.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Wet and windy Thanksgiving Day\n\nWidespread soaking of 1 to 2 inches of rainfall expected on \nThanksgiving Day as a very strong easterly low level flow \nassociated with a powerful coastal storm system transports \nanomalous moisture into CPA. Model consensus timing suggests \nprecip crosses the MD line around or shortly after 12Z/7AM \nThursday, but there is still some uncertainty regarding start \ntime. The heaviest rain is currently projected to fall between \n7AM Thu and 1AM Fri. \n\nThe potential for strong wind gusts remains a concern with this\nsystem, particularly over the southeastern third of the CWA. \nRecent runs of some of the deterministic models, including the \nGFS and ECMWF, have shown a 60 to 90 knot 850 mb low-level \neasterly jet developing in response to the strong pressure \ngradient between the deepening low to the south and retreating \nhigh pressure to the north. It remains to be seen how much of \nthis wind will be able to make it down to the surface, but \nsoundings from the GFS show 45-50 knot winds at the top of the \nmixed layer Thursday afternoon over the Lower Susquehanna \nValley. Probabilities of reaching 60 mph gusts were greater \nthan 50 percent for areas in Lancaster County, which warranted \nthe issuing of a High Wind Watch. The Laurel Highlands are \nexpected to see gusts up to 45 mph Thursday through Saturday, \nwhile much of the southeastern third of PA is expected to see \n35-50 mph gusts. The need for wind advisories will be evaluated \nlater tonight. Elsewhere in PA will see 25-35 mph gusts. Timing \nof strongest winds will be Thursday afternoon. Of note, the \nstrong winds on Thursday will be from the east which anecdotally\ndoes tend to cause more tree damage since trees are \"tested\" a \nlot less frequently from the east than from the north or west in\nthis region. Unfortunately, isolated to scattered power outages\nwill be possible on Thanksgiving, especially in the Lower Susq \nValley where winds are strongest. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Breezy on Friday with rain showers/elevation snow \n\nColder air will gradually wrap back into Pennsylvania late \nThursday night and Friday as the system stacks up along the\nNorth-Central Appalachians. This should allow for snow to begin\nmixing in especially over the higher terrain from the Laurel\nHighlands northward along the Allegheny Plateau into northern\nPA. Rain/snow mix during the day Friday beneath the stacked low\ntransitions to more of a lake effect/upslope precip pattern \nFriday night into Saturday as the system lifts into New England.\nIt will remain breezy into the holiday weekend with west winds \ngusting 25-35 mph.",
